DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ddeevviinnffoo library provides access to the kernel's internal device hier‐
     archy and to the I/O resource manager.  The library uses a sysctl(3) in‐
     terface to obtain a snapshot of the kernel's state, which is then made
     available to the application.

     Due to the fact that the information may be logically arranged in a num‐
     ber of different fashions, the library does not attempt to impose any
     structure on the data.

     Device, resource, and resource manager information is returned in data
     structures defined in <_d_e_v_i_n_f_o_._h>:

           struct devinfo_dev {
               devinfo_handle_t    dd_handle;      /* device handle */
               devinfo_handle_t    dd_parent;      /* parent handle */
               char                *dd_name;       /* name of device */
               char                *dd_desc;       /* device description */
               char                *dd_drivername; /* name of attached driver */
               char                *dd_pnpinfo;    /* pnp info from parent bus */
               char                *dd_location;   /* Where bus thinks dev at */
               uint32_t            dd_devflags;    /* API flags */
               uint16_t            dd_flags;       /* internal dev flags */
               device_state_t      dd_state;       /* attachment state of dev */
           };

           struct devinfo_rman {
               devinfo_handle_t    dm_handle;      /* resource manager handle */
               rman_res_t          dm_start;       /* resource start */
               rman_res_t          dm_size;        /* resource size */
               char                *dm_desc;       /* resource description */
           };

           struct devinfo_res {
               devinfo_handle_t    dr_handle;      /* resource handle */
               devinfo_handle_t    dr_rman;        /* resource manager handle */
               devinfo_handle_t    dr_device;      /* owning device */
               rman_res_t          dr_start;       /* region start */
               rman_res_t          dr_size;        /* region size */
           };

     The _d_e_v_i_n_f_o___h_a_n_d_l_e___t values can be used to look up the correspondingly
     referenced structures.

     ddeevviinnffoo__iinniitt() takes a snapshot of the kernel's internal device and re‐
     source state.  It returns nonzero if after a number of retries a consis‐
     tent snapshot cannot be obtained.  ddeevviinnffoo__iinniitt() must be called before
     any other functions can be used.

     ddeevviinnffoo__ffrreeee() releases the memory associated with the snapshot.  Any
     pointers returned by other functions are invalidated by this, and
     ddeevviinnffoo__iinniitt() must be called again before using any other functions.

     ddeevviinnffoo__hhaannddllee__ttoo__ddeevviiccee(), ddeevviinnffoo__hhaannddllee__ttoo__rreessoouurrccee() and
     ddeevviinnffoo__hhaannddllee__ttoo__rrmmaann() return pointers to _d_e_v_i_n_f_o___d_e_v, _d_e_v_i_n_f_o___r_e_s and
     _d_e_v_i_n_f_o___r_m_a_n structures respectively based on the _d_e_v_i_n_f_o___h_a_n_d_l_e___t passed
     to them.  These functions can be used to traverse the tree from any node
     to any other node.  If ddeevviinnffoo__hhaannddllee__ttoo__ddeevviiccee() is passed the constant
     DEVINFO_ROOT_DEVICE it will return the handle to the root of the device
     tree.

     ddeevviinnffoo__ffoorreeaacchh__ddeevviiccee__cchhiilldd() invokes its callback argument _f_n on every
     device which is an immediate child of _d_e_v_i_c_e.  The _f_n function is also
     passed _a_r_g, allowing state to be passed to the callback function.  If _f_n
     returns a nonzero error value the traversal is halted, and
     ddeevviinnffoo__ffoorreeaacchh__ddeevviiccee__cchhiilldd() returns the error value to its caller.

     ddeevviinnffoo__ffoorreeaacchh__ddeevviiccee__rreessoouurrccee() invokes its callback argument _f_n on ev‐
     ery resource which is owned by _d_e_v_i_c_e.  The _f_n function is also passed
     _d_e_v_i_c_e and _a_r_g, allowing state to be passed to the callback function.  If
     _f_n returns a nonzero error value the traversal is halted, and
     ddeevviinnffoo__ffoorreeaacchh__ddeevviiccee__rreessoouurrccee() returns the error value to its caller.

     ddeevviinnffoo__ffoorreeaacchh__rrmmaann__rreessoouurrccee() invokes its callback argument _f_n on every
     resource within the resource manager _r_m_a_n.  The _f_n function is also
     passed _a_r_g, allowing state to be passed to the callback function.  If _f_n
     returns a nonzero error value the traversal is halted, and
     ddeevviinnffoo__ffoorreeaacchh__rrmmaann__rreessoouurrccee() returns the error value to its caller.

     ddeevviinnffoo__ffoorreeaacchh__rrmmaann() invokes its callback argument _f_n on every resource
     manager.  The _f_n function is also passed _a_r_g, allowing state to be passed
